  "slug": "genomic-conversion-2e77",
  "summary": "Recent preprints test molecular readouts against conventional census data for coral and plankton communities.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of molecular and community-level data to infer ecological responses in marine and freshwater taxa. Seawater eDNA metabarcoding at Mermaid Reef tracks coral community restructuring after a 2025 heatwave and matches photo-transect cover and assemblage patterns. A 20-year eukaryotic plankton metabarcoding series from the Western English Channel documents a transient 2008-2011 biodiversity shift, while a global comparison across occurrence, abundance, biomass and metagenomic data reports a latitudinal gradient in open-ocean plankton diversity. Two remaining items address different questions: a meta-analysis shows sublethal chemical exposure lowers heat tolerance and increases its variability in freshwater invertebrates and fish, and a comparison of anuran taxonomic and functional diversity contrasts oil-contaminated and reference Choco streams. What is new is the explicit cross-checking of molecular readouts against conventional census methods, most clearly in the coral and plankton studies.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
